1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,044
Lesson 0.7.

2
00:00:02,424 --> 00:00:03,838
JAX essentials.

3
00:00:04,950 --> 00:00:10,028
Every lab in the course is programmed
in JAX, and the thermodynamic

4
00:00:10,028 --> 00:00:14,652
hardware simulators, such as
THRML and Torx, are built on it.

5
00:00:15,032 --> 00:00:18,788
JAX is NumPy with three superpowers:
function transformations

6
00:00:18,788 --> 00:00:22,236
that vectorize, compile and
differentiate automatically.

7
00:00:22,616 --> 00:00:26,706
Its functional style fits
physics: a simulation step takes a

8
00:00:26,706 --> 00:00:30,455
state and returns the next
one, with no hidden effects.

9
00:00:31,583 --> 00:00:34,298
A pure function is a
box that depends only on

10
00:00:34,298 --> 00:00:36,710
its inputs and only produces its output.

11
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:39,660
It does not read changing
global variables, does

12
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:42,015
not print, does not
modify anything outside.

13
00:00:42,395 --> 00:00:45,428
Call it twice with the same
thing and you get the same thing.

14
00:00:45,808 --> 00:00:49,185
That discipline is what lets
JAX transform your functions

15
00:00:49,185 --> 00:00:52,739
safely: it can trace, compile
and differentiate them because

16
00:00:52,739 --> 00:00:55,641
it knows exactly what goes
in and what comes out.

17
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:57,858
And randomness?

18
00:00:58,238 --> 00:01:01,094
In NumPy, the generator
keeps a hidden state

19
00:01:01,094 --> 00:01:04,015
that changes on every
call: that is not pure.

20
00:01:04,395 --> 00:01:07,191
JAX makes that state explicit: a key.

21
00:01:07,571 --> 00:01:10,359
The same key always
produces the same numbers.

22
00:01:10,739 --> 00:01:14,158
To get new numbers, you
split the key, which

23
00:01:14,158 --> 00:01:17,810
creates independent child
keys, forming a tree.

24
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:21,275
Each branch of the tree feeds
one part of the program.

25
00:01:21,655 --> 00:01:24,352
Golden rule: never reuse a key.

26
00:01:25,483 --> 00:01:30,103
Write a function for a single particle,
for example one Langevin step.

27
00:01:30,483 --> 00:01:33,893
With vmap, JAX applies it to
a thousand particles at once,

28
00:01:33,893 --> 00:01:37,126
adding a batch axis, without
you writing a single loop.

29
00:01:37,506 --> 00:01:40,403
The result is the same
as calling the function a

30
00:01:40,403 --> 00:01:43,782
thousand times, but it runs
as one vectorized operation.

31
00:01:44,162 --> 00:01:49,877
They can be nested: vmap over particles
and, on top, vmap over temperatures.

32
00:01:50,983 --> 00:01:55,539
The first time you call a jit-decorated
function, JAX runs it with

33
00:01:55,539 --> 00:01:59,680
abstract values, records every
operation and builds a graph.

34
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:04,162
That graph is handed to the
XLA compiler, which fuses

35
00:02:04,162 --> 00:02:09,115
operations and generates optimized
code for the CPU, GPU or TPU.

36
00:02:09,495 --> 00:02:14,194
Later calls with the same input
shape reuse the compiled code.

37
00:02:14,574 --> 00:02:19,593
The cost is an initial compilation;
the payoff, much faster executions.

38
00:02:20,717 --> 00:02:23,827
Simulations are loops:
today's state produces

39
00:02:23,827 --> 00:02:27,766
tomorrow's. scan expresses
that loop in a compilable way.

40
00:02:28,146 --> 00:02:31,923
It takes a function that receives
a carry, the state, and a

41
00:02:31,923 --> 00:02:35,508
per-step input, and returns
the new carry and an output.

42
00:02:35,888 --> 00:02:40,470
Here the carry is a particle's
position and the input is one key per

43
00:02:40,470 --> 00:02:44,716
step: it is the Euler–Maruyama
method for an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck

44
00:02:44,716 --> 00:02:49,097
process. scan stacks the outputs
and returns the full trajectory.

45
00:02:50,217 --> 00:02:53,708
For a function with n
inputs and m outputs, the

46
00:02:53,708 --> 00:02:56,754
derivative is the m-by-n Jacobian matrix.

47
00:02:57,134 --> 00:02:58,536
We almost never form it.

48
00:02:58,916 --> 00:03:04,454
The Jacobian–vector product, jvp,
pushes an input direction forward and

49
00:03:04,454 --> 00:03:09,680
gives a combination of columns: it
is forward-mode differentiation.

50
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:15,539
The vector–Jacobian product, vjp,
pulls an output direction backward and

51
00:03:15,539 --> 00:03:20,486
gives a combination of rows: it
is reverse mode, backpropagation.

52
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:25,316
For a scalar loss, the gradient is a
single vjp, which is why training networks

53
00:03:25,316 --> 00:03:29,203
with millions of parameters costs
almost the same as evaluating them.

54
00:03:30,317 --> 00:03:31,829
Let's check with an example.

55
00:03:32,209 --> 00:03:36,028
Let f(x) = sin(x) x².

56
00:03:36,408 --> 00:03:41,426
Its derivative is cosine of x times
x², plus two x times sine of x.

57
00:03:41,806 --> 00:03:48,020
At x = 1 it equals cosine of
one plus two times sine of one,

58
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:53,923
about 2.22. jax.grad returns
exactly that number, 2.2232,

59
00:03:54,303 --> 00:03:57,165
with no finite differences
or truncation errors.

60
00:03:58,283 --> 00:04:01,307
In lab one you will
generate a million Bernoulli

61
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:04,078
samples with vmap and
measure their entropy.

62
00:04:04,458 --> 00:04:08,999
From lab three on you will simulate
stochastic dynamics with scan and jit.

63
00:04:09,379 --> 00:04:13,064
In labs ten and eleven you
will use jvp and vjp to

64
00:04:13,064 --> 00:04:16,896
multiply by curvature
matrices without forming them.

65
00:04:17,276 --> 00:04:23,100
And in unit eleven you will work with
THRML and Torx, which are written in JAX.

66
00:04:24,217 --> 00:04:26,514
These pieces reappear
throughout the course:

67
00:04:26,894 --> 00:04:32,544
Euler–Maruyama in unit two, chromatic
Gibbs sampling as a vmap over

68
00:04:32,544 --> 00:04:38,785
same-color nodes in unit four,
Gauss–Newton–vector products in unit eight,

69
00:04:39,165 --> 00:04:44,314
and the simulation of thermodynamic
chips with THRML in unit eleven.

70
00:04:45,417 --> 00:04:49,692
Classic mistakes: reusing a key
and getting repeated numbers.

71
00:04:50,072 --> 00:04:54,201
Putting side effects inside jit,
which only happen while tracing.

72
00:04:54,581 --> 00:04:58,007
Using a Python if that
depends on an array value,

73
00:04:58,007 --> 00:05:00,874
instead of JAX's control-flow primitives.

74
00:05:01,254 --> 00:05:04,017
And mixing NumPy's generator with JAX's.

75
00:05:05,117 --> 00:05:10,346
In summary: in JAX functions are
pure and randomness travels in

76
00:05:10,346 --> 00:05:15,575
explicit keys. vmap vectorizes,
jit compiles and scan iterates.

77
00:05:15,575 --> 00:05:20,224
jvp pushes derivatives forward,
vjp pulls them backward,

78
00:05:20,604 --> 00:05:24,567
and the gradient of a
scalar loss is a single vjp.

79
00:05:25,683 --> 00:05:29,470
To close unit zero: write a
function that reproducibly

80
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:33,747
generates a million normals and
computes their mean with jit.

81
00:05:34,127 --> 00:05:38,225
Explain the difference between a
gradient and a Jacobian–vector product.

82
00:05:38,605 --> 00:05:40,970
Predict the output shape of a vmap.

83
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:46,878
And count how many jvps or vjps
are needed to form a full Jacobian.
